# Byte stability for hashed content.
#
# This repository records sha256 hashes of its own files as evidence. Git's end-of-line
# normalization (core.autocrlf=true, the Git for Windows default) rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout, which changes the bytes and therefore the hash. Any path whose content is hashed
# anywhere in this repository MUST be listed here with -text, or the hash is wrong on every
# Windows clone while remaining right in the repository, which is the hardest version of this
# bug to see.

# bench/corpus/** is generator output written UTF-8, no BOM, LF, exactly
# one trailing newline (bench/generator/README.md, "Output bytes"). Without
# this line, git normalizes line endings on checkout and every recorded
# artifact_sha256 is wrong on Windows. Required for S-03 AC-1; see
# bench/README.md, "Prerequisites".
bench/corpus/** -text

# Skill example artifacts are hashed into expected_envelope.run.artifact_sha256 by every
# skills/*/examples/*.json fixture. This rule was missing until v0.1.x, and the consequence was
# not hypothetical: all 22 recorded hashes were already wrong on a Windows working tree, matching
# the LF form stored in git and never the CRLF form on disk. The whole examples tree is covered
# rather than only today's artifact paths, so a skill that puts a hashed artifact somewhere new
# does not silently reintroduce the defect.
# The glob is recursive so a nested fixture tree (skills/_template-fixture/critique-toy/examples/)
# is covered on the same terms as a top-level one.
# Guarded by scripts/tests/test_example_artifact_hashes.py.
skills/**/examples/** -text
